Rules for interpreting the metrics
Compare the same periods, plan types and return frequencies.

Use total-return benchmarks where appropriate, not price-only indices that exclude dividends.

Use rolling-return distributions rather than one favourable start and end date.

Examine performance under different market conditions, including corrections and recoveries.

Attribute performance to the actual manager and investment process in place during the period.

Separate genuine evidence of skill from favourable market exposure, sector bets, style cycles and luck.

A fund with lower returns may still be preferable if it has materially lower downside risk and better fits your needs. Conversely, lower volatility is not automatically better if it results from a strategy that fails its investment objective.

