A 15% decline can create an opportunity, but it can also signal a deteriorating portfolio. We must establish which is more plausible before investing extra money.

9. Future market conditions and risk–reward scenarios
This is where your framework moves beyond historical fund rankings.

Whenever we evaluate a fund for investment, we will build three forward-looking scenarios. These will be conditional estimates, not promises or precise market predictions.

Bull scenario
Earnings growth is strong, valuations remain supportive or expand, and liquidity conditions improve.

We will estimate: plausible upside, the assumptions required to achieve it, and whether the fund is positioned to participate.

Base scenario
Earnings and valuations evolve without a major positive or negative surprise.

We will estimate: a reasonable return range using earnings assumptions, valuation changes, income where relevant and costs.

Bear scenario
Earnings disappoint, valuations contract, interest rates rise unexpectedly or credit and liquidity risks emerge.

We will estimate: plausible downside, possible recovery challenges and whether you could still meet your financial goal.

For equity funds, we will consider earnings growth, valuation multiples, sector exposures and market conditions. For debt funds, we'll consider interest-rate changes, duration, credit spreads, defaults and liquidity.

Where the data supports it, we'll also use historical stress periods and sensitivity analysis. We will clearly distinguish historical observations from model assumptions and future estimates.

10. Your opportunity-fund strategy
Your idea of accumulating money in debt funds and deploying it during market corrections is worth examining, but it needs explicit rules.

The most important distinction is between your emergency reserve and your investment opportunity reserve. They must not be mixed.

A practical framework is:

Emergency reserve: Keep it available for genuine emergencies. Do not expose it to equity-market risk.

Regular investments: Continue the planned SIP according to your long-term allocation.

Opportunity reserve: Use only surplus money that can remain uninvested without compromising your goals.

Correction trigger: Use market valuation, underlying fundamentals, drawdown and target asset allocation together.

Deployment rule: Invest in predetermined stages instead of attempting to identify the exact market bottom.

Maximum waiting period: Set a review date, such as 12 months, so that the opportunity reserve does not become a permanent market-timing strategy.

For short-term opportunity money, we will compare overnight, liquid and money-market funds, as well as suitable bank deposits. We will assess liquidity, credit exposure, NAV volatility, redemption conditions and applicable taxation before selecting a vehicle.

We will not assume that a debt mutual fund guarantees the capital value of your reserve.

11. How we will use the framework for an actual fund
Here is the repeatable process I propose.

Define the investment role. Establish the objective, horizon, acceptable loss and target allocation.

Create the eligible universe. Identify comparable schemes in the same category and plan type.

Apply mandatory filters. Remove unsuitable schemes and investigate material red flags.

Collect current evidence. Review official scheme documents, latest portfolio holdings, expense ratio, NAV history, benchmark data and relevant market conditions.

Calculate comparable metrics. Analyse rolling returns, risk-adjusted performance, drawdowns, valuation, manager tenure and portfolio overlap.

Score and compare. Apply the weighted scorecard to the shortlisted funds and explain material differences.

Model risk and reward. Develop bull, base and bear scenarios with transparent assumptions.

Make the decision. Recommend invest, shortlist, hold, or avoid, and explain the appropriate role and allocation.

Set monitoring conditions. Establish review triggers for manager changes, strategy drift, persistent underperformance, rising risk, valuation changes and portfolio imbalance.

